<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jutes</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jutes were a Germanic people who, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>, came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> and settled in Kent, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-wight">Isle of Wight</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a> in the 5th century, alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>. Kent's royal house traced its origin to the Jutish leaders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hengist-and-horsa">Hengist and Horsa</a>. Archaeology shows close links between early Kent, Jutland and Frankish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, and the Jutes soon merged into the wider English population.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jutes.jpg" alt="Jutes" width="320" height="578" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LibIchtnatz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jutland_Peninsula_map.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
